# {{ .ProviderShortName }} Provider

The `badgerops/unifi` provider manages **declarative UniFi Network site configuration** through Ubiquiti's official **Integration API** (API-key authentication).

It is built for configuration state: networks, WiFi broadcasts, zone-based firewall, DNS policy, traffic matching lists, ACL rules, DHCP reservations, and port forwards. It is **not** a day-2 operations tool (no device adoption, client telemetry, firmware upgrades, or analytics).

-> **New here?** Read [Getting started](docs/guides/getting-started). Migrating from `paultyng/unifi` or `ubiquiti-community/unifi`? See [Compared to legacy UniFi providers](docs/guides/vs-legacy-providers).

-> **Pull requests are welcome.** Docs fixes, examples, repros, and focused resource improvements all help — [open a PR](https://github.com/BadgerOps/terraform-provider-unifi/pulls) or [start with an issue](https://github.com/BadgerOps/terraform-provider-unifi/issues).

For opt-in prerelease testing, see [Testing release candidates](docs/guides/release-candidates).

## Who this is for

- Operators who want Terraform to own site configuration on current UniFi Network releases
- Controllers that expose the Integration API (local console / UDM, or UniFi Site Manager cloud connector)
- Teams that prefer API-key auth over a local-admin username/password against the private controller API

## Who this is not for

- Device adoption, firmware upgrades, client blocking, reconnect/locate, or live telemetry
- Controllers with no Integration API / API keys
- Expecting full parity with every UniFi UI control — the provider tracks a committed Integration OpenAPI snapshot, plus a few documented legacy-API exceptions below

## Authentication and connection

1. In the UniFi Network UI, open **Settings → Control Plane → Integrations** (labels vary by Network version).
2. Enable the Integration API if needed and create an **API key**. Store it outside git (CI secret, encrypted tfvars, etc.).
3. Set `api_url` to the **base** console URL. The provider appends `/integration` when that path is missing.

| Access path | Example `api_url` |
| --- | --- |
| Local console / UDM | `https://192.0.2.1` or `https://unifi.example.com` |
| UniFi Site Manager cloud connector | `https://api.ui.com/v1/connector/consoles/<console-id>/network` |

Optional provider settings:

| Argument | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `api_key` (required, sensitive) | Integration API key from the UniFi Network UI |
| `api_url` (required) | Base controller URL (see table above) |
| `allow_insecure` | Skip TLS verify — lab / self-signed only |
| `request_timeout_seconds` | Per-request HTTP timeout (default `30`). Raise it when UniFi stops answering during device reprovision after WiFi or similar changes; otherwise applies can fail mid-run with `context deadline exceeded` |

~> There are **no** provider-level environment variable defaults for `api_url` / `api_key` today. Pass them in the `provider` block (or via Terraform variable interpolation from your secret store).

## Site model

Almost every resource and data source is **site-scoped**. Resolve the site once, then pass `site_id` everywhere:

```terraform
data "unifi_site" "main" {
  name = "Default" # or your site name
}
```

There is no provider-level `site` argument (unlike many legacy UniFi providers). Multi-site controllers use multiple `data.unifi_site` lookups or hard-coded IDs.

## Compatibility

- Tracks the committed UniFi Network Integration OpenAPI snapshot **10.6.106**
- Newer Network application builds usually work against that surface; snapshot refreshes are noted in the changelog
- Implementation translates Integration DTOs explicitly — no raw JSON passthrough on managed resources

